WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.170
Actors have to constantly ponder their next
moves.

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:05.610
What if a role turns out to be nothing like
they thought?

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.660
Can they speak openly about regret?

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.580
If they do, will they ever work again?

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:14.121
The following TV actors regretted some aspect
of their work—a poorly shot scene, a show

00:00:14.121 --> 00:00:18.260
they wish had gone differently, a job they
wouldn't take if they had to do it over again—and

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:21.109
admitted their disappointment later on.

00:00:21.109 --> 00:00:22.640
In the weeds

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.830
Mary-Louise Parker has filmed a number of
steamy scenes in her long career, and more

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:30.040
than one in her role as Nancy Botwin on Showtime's
Weeds.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:31.529
"Worked for me so far."

00:00:31.529 --> 00:00:36.941
But for one 2008 episode, Parker shot a revealing
bathtub scene we can't show here, that she

00:00:36.941 --> 00:00:38.589
later wished she could take back.

00:00:38.589 --> 00:00:39.879
She told More magazine:

00:00:39.879 --> 00:00:42.829
"I fought with the director about it, and
now I'm bitter.

00:00:42.829 --> 00:00:43.969
[...] I wish I hadn’t done that.

00:00:43.969 --> 00:00:45.329
I was goaded into it."

00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:49.719
She later clarified that the "fight" was really
just a creative discussion, and that she's

00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:53.289
totally comfortable being in her birthday
suit on camera and off, saying,

00:00:53.289 --> 00:00:57.129
"I was talking about that scene as an example
of something I didn't love as much when I

00:00:57.129 --> 00:00:58.799
saw it."

00:00:58.799 --> 00:01:00.249
Dark turn

00:01:00.249 --> 00:01:05.359
Devon Gummersall is best known for playing
Brian Krakow, the big-haired, Angela Chase-obsessed

00:01:05.359 --> 00:01:09.090
nerd on the short-lived '90s drama My So-Called
Life.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:13.549
"Finally: Ane rection from actual physical
contact."

00:01:13.549 --> 00:01:18.310
Gummersall would go on to portray Stevie Wolcott
on Mad Men, but before he was Peggy Olson's

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:23.689
blind date, he played a very bad dude named
Zach on the Keri Russell-led WB serial Felicity.

00:01:23.689 --> 00:01:28.210
At the time, Gummersall was into the role
because it was such a departure from previous

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:29.210
characters.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:32.750
In retrospect, he later told Cosmopolitan
he sees it as a mistake.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:36.829
"I loved being on the show, but in the end,
I realized that it was sort of a mistake to

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:38.330
do that kind of a storyline.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:41.869
I would have much rather been on the show
more long-term and done something that didn't

00:01:41.869 --> 00:01:44.170
have to end in such a bad way."

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.939
Unhealthy Minds

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:51.330
Mandy Patinkin, who played Agent Jason Gideon
on CBS's Criminal Minds for two seasons, quit

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:55.210
the procedural in 2007 after not attending
a table read.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:59.130
Patinkin later said he regretted making the
decision to do the show in the first place,

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.350
due to its graphic and violent nature.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.609
Patinkin told New York Magazine in 2012:

00:02:03.609 --> 00:02:06.810
"It was very destructive to my soul and my
personality."

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:11.430
Patinkin, who now plays Saul on Showtime's
Homeland, explained what he sees as the difference

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:12.760
between the two shows.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:15.660
Of Homeland, which itself isn't without violence,
Patinkin said,

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:19.140
"It asks why there's a need for violence in
the first place."

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.840
Friends like these

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:26.510
For five episodes in 1999, Friends saw the
addition of Janine, played by model Elle Macpherson,

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:30.489
who moved into Joey's apartment and briefly
became his love interest after Chandler moved

00:02:30.489 --> 00:02:32.349
across the hall to live with Monica.

00:02:32.349 --> 00:02:36.700
Most actors would have killed to extend their
arc on the wildly popular show, but Macpherson

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:41.340
turned down the opportunity, saying that had
she known the show would have such staying

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.519
power, she wouldn't have taken the job in
the first place.

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:47.681
"If I'd known how important it was in the
U.S. or how long it would be on TV, I may

00:02:47.681 --> 00:02:49.040
not have chosen to do it."

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:54.349
While the show proved career-making for Macpherson's
castmates, she's spent the last 20 years reminding

00:02:54.349 --> 00:02:57.430
people she had a career before—and after—Janine.

00:02:57.430 --> 00:02:58.800
O.C. overkill

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:04.390
Mischa Barton played Marissa Cooper on Fox's
The O.C., a drama about beautiful, wealthy

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:08.480
teens that ran for four seasons—although
Cooper was written off in a car accident in

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.410
the show at the end of season 3.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:14.630
Since leaving the show, Barton has been open
about her regrets, telling the UK's Metro

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:15.630
in 2014:

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.709
"It's something I came so close to not doing.

00:03:17.709 --> 00:03:19.990
I had a really great thing with film.

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:23.159
People say be grateful for what you have but
it's certainly not the kind of thing I was

00:03:23.159 --> 00:03:24.560
expecting it to be."

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:29.180
The O.C. plunged all of its young stars into
the spotlight, which Barton particularly resented.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:33.870
"I just like to be seen for the hard-working
actress that I am and not for a bunch of extraneous

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:35.290
press."

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:36.969
Community feud

00:03:36.969 --> 00:03:41.270
After being out of the entertainment spotlight
for years, Chevy Chase returned to television

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:44.090
in 2009 as part of NBC's Community.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:49.160
In 2013, during the critically lauded but
perennially ratings-starved show's fourth

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:53.620
season, Chase left, calling his decision to
join the cast "a big mistake," citing the

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:58.230
exhaustion of doing long television hours
and calling sitcoms, quote, "the lowest form

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.260
of television."

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:03.849
Chase and the show's creator, Dan Harmon,
were also engaged in a well-documented feud.

00:04:03.849 --> 00:04:05.599
"Ok, thanks for coming.

00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:06.599
Bye bye!"

00:04:06.599 --> 00:04:07.790
Thanks for watching!

